  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways a rod-shaped microscopic swimmer or active particle can move and stir its surroundings. Across the top row, the rod rotates and this rotation drives different patterns on nearby surfaces or interfaces, shown in blue: simple back-and-forth in-plane motion, circulating motion, and localized vertical pumping. The red curved arrows indicate the particle’s rotational degrees of freedom, while the blue arrows show the resulting motion induced in the adjacent planes.\n\nThe middle row shifts to motion in bulk fluid. One case shows a rod translating through an otherwise uniform flow, and another shows it placed in a nonuniform or extensional flow field, where the surrounding streamlines bend around it. These panels emphasize how imposed flow and particle orientation interact.\n\nThe bottom row combines these ideas into more complex environments. The rod couples two nearby interfaces or objects, appears to mix or transfer motion between them, and can also be driven by multiple directional inputs from different sides. The final panel suggests that an external flow field together with a localized incoming stream can reorient or propel the rod. Overall, the image compares distinct hydrodynamic actuation and coupling modes for an elongated active object near boundaries and in flowing media.",
